 Failure before the successor holds the role (start refused, no valid answer within `sessionRotation.answerWaitMs`): `rotation.json` is removed first (a late `session answered` is then refused), then Identity is asked once. If it already names the successor as holder, the rotation is finished and the incident `rotation-completed` says it was finished after the deadline; if Identity cannot answer nothing is ended and the incident says so; only otherwise the successor is ended the same way. After the ended successor's attempt has closed, Identity is asked who holds the role (invariant: after any outcome exactly one live head holds it): if nobody live does, a take that landed late hit the dead successor, and the old head registers the role again through its own supervisor (verb `session restore role`, registered without `take`, so Identity refuses when any live session holds it) with the incident `role-restored`; if that fails too the incident is `no-live-head` and the executor stops. In that last case the old session stays alive and keeps its placement record, one incident line goes to `incidents.jsonl`, and the reason is returned. Nothing is ever killed with SIGKILL: ending a session is its supervisor's normal stop. The next `session start` fronts whichever session Identity says holds the role.
 
+**An executor that died is resolved on the normal path.** Before `session rotate` takes its normal rotation lock, and before `session answered` records an answer, the pending record's `executor` is judged by the process door (pid and start time, with host/boot/namespace identity). Only `dead` permits recovery; `alive`, `unknown`, and older records without this field are never guessed dead or overwritten. Recovery takes the same lineage lock without waiting and rereads the record. Identity alone then decides: if it names the successor (its head generation, terminal key, or matching recorded answer), placement follows the successor and the predecessor is asked to stop; otherwise the successor is asked to stop and the rotation is abandoned. The predecessor snapshot is saved before launch; the returned successor placement is saved after launch. A crash between launch and that save reads the successor assignment's newest run envelope through the existing assignment-layout reader. Successful resolution removes `rotation.json` and appends one `resolved-orphan` incident with outcome `completed` or `abandoned`; a late answer is then `no-rotation-pending`. An unavailable Identity, an unlocated successor or an unwritable stop request retains the record for retry and ends nothing on a guessed holder. A new rotate request proceeds only after resolution; an unreclaimed pending record answers `rotation-in-progress`. Doctor's `session-rotation-orphans` is one read-only pointer: it counts proven-dead executors within its 250 ms budget (otherwise omits the count), warns, never fails or repairs, and points to the lineage's next rotate or answer.
